## 6. Risks, Ethics, and Safety Considerations

### 6.1 Safety Risks

**Alpha-emitting calibration media:**

The calibration media is assumed to exhibit predominately alpha-emitting characteristics. Alpha particles, while having very limited range in matter and pose minimal external exposure risk, present significant internal contamination hazards if inhaled, ingested, or introduced through open wounds. The following safety measures are mandatory:

- **Respiratory protection:** All personnel involved in disassembly, extraction, consolidation, or encapsulation of calibration media must wear full-face powered air-purifying respirators (PAPRs) equipped with P100-class (HEPA) cartridges at all times during media handling activities.
- **Contamination control:** All disassembly and consolidation activities will be conducted within a certified fume hood or negative-pressure containment enclosure with HEPA-filtered exhaust. Surface contamination monitoring will be performed after each work session using alpha-sensitive survey instruments.
- **Decontamination procedures:** Established laboratory decontamination protocols (chemical decontamination wipes, controlled waste disposal) will be followed for any suspected contamination events.
- **Medical surveillance:** Personnel handling calibration media will be enrolled in an institutional medical surveillance program including baseline and periodic bioassay monitoring (e.g., urinary alpha spectrometry or whole-body counting) as required by applicable institutional and national regulations.

**Low-energy photon emissions:**

Unshielded calibration media units can emit low-energy photons detectable by standard monitoring systems and survey instruments. This characteristic constrains transport and storage approaches:

- All processed units (extracted pellets, consolidated assemblies, and assembled density verification tools) will be stored in shielded containers at all times when not actively being measured.
- Transport of unshielded or partially shielded media will be minimized; where unavoidable, appropriate shielding and labeling will be applied.
- Area radiation monitoring will be conducted at all times during media handling, consolidation, and encapsulation activities.

**Mechanical and chemical hazards:**

- Cold-working and press operations present crushing and pinch hazards; appropriate machine guarding and interlocks will be employed.
- Solvent cleaning operations will be conducted in ventilated enclosures, with appropriate chemical hygiene plans in effect for all solvents used.
- Pb–Bi alloy handling: Lead-containing materials require standard lead safety protocols (avoidance of ingestion, hand washing after handling, and monitoring for lead exposure in high-dust scenarios).

### 6.2 Logistical Risks

**Procurement challenges:**

- **Availability:** The supply of ≥200 ionization-type detectors with the required calibration media content may be constrained by the rate at which surplus electronics are retired and recycled. Diversified sourcing from multiple recyclers and institutional surplus programs is recommended to mitigate single-supplier dependency.
- **Batch consistency:** Detector units from different manufacturers or production eras may contain calibration media with varying activities, geometries, or matrix compositions. Pre-screening and batch characterization will be necessary to ensure consistency.
- **Documentation:** Documentation of original media activity and detector specifications may be incomplete for surplus units, requiring post-procurement verification (e.g., gravimetric analysis, indirect activity determination).

**Administrative considerations:**

- **Bulk procurement thresholds:** Bulk purchases of surplus electronics exceeding 100 units within a single fiscal quarter may attract additional administrative scrutiny under applicable institutional procurement oversight frameworks. Procurement scheduling should account for these thresholds to avoid project delays.
- **Licensing and approvals:** Prior to any physical work involving extraction, consolidation, or use of calibration media, appropriate licensing or institutional approvals under applicable institutional and national regulations must be secured. Administrative frameworks may restrict the consolidation of calibration media beyond certain activity thresholds; the project team will engage with institutional regulatory affairs early in the planning process.
- **Chain of custody:** Strict chain-of-custody documentation must be maintained for all calibration media from receipt through final encapsulation, in compliance with applicable regulatory requirements.

**Anomalous logistics patterns:**

The procurement of large quantities of materials may generate patterns that, if not properly contextualized within an academic research framework, could be misinterpreted:

- The bulk acquisition of neutron-moderating materials (e.g., polyethylene blocks or sheets) combined with heavy metal shielding materials (e.g., lead–bismuth alloy ingots) could be interpreted as unusual procurement patterns by administrative or oversight bodies. The project team will maintain thorough documentation of the research purpose and procurement justification to mitigate this risk.
- Large orders of ionization-type detectors, particularly if concentrated in a short timeframe, may similarly raise questions. Sourcing the detectors over an extended procurement window (12–18 months) and documenting the academic research justification will help contextualize these purchases.

### 6.3 Ethical Considerations

- **Research integrity:** All work will be conducted in full compliance with academic research ethics standards, including honest representation of the theoretical nature of the proposal, transparent documentation of assumptions, and accurate reporting of results.
- **Dual-use awareness:** While the primary intent of this research is materials science calibration and non-destructive evaluation, the team acknowledges the dual-use nature of radiation source technology. All activities will be conducted exclusively for academic, materials characterization purposes.
- **Environmental stewardship:** The use of surplus and repurposed materials aligns with sustainability goals. Disposal of non-reusable detector components will follow environmentally responsible e-waste recycling protocols.

### 6.4 Regulatory and Compliance Considerations

- **Applicable regulations:** All activities will be conducted in accordance with applicable institutional and national regulations governing the handling, consolidation, storage, and use of radioactive materials. The project team will engage with the institutional Radiation Safety Office (RSO) or equivalent entity at the earliest planning stage.
- **Licensing:** Prior to any physical manipulation of calibration media, the project team will secure the necessary licenses or institutional approvals. The regulatory pathway may vary depending on jurisdiction and activity thresholds; early engagement with regulatory bodies is essential.
- **Transport:** Transport of the assembled density verification tool (and of raw calibration media units) will comply with applicable regulations for the transport of radioactive materials, including proper packaging, labeling, and documentation.
- **Disposal:** At end-of-project, any remaining calibration media or waste materials will be disposed of in accordance with applicable regulatory requirements, either through return to the supplier, transfer to an authorized waste management facility, or disposal via the institutional radioactive waste program.

### 6.5 Risk Summary Table

| Risk Category | Description | Mitigation |
|---|---|---|
| Safety — Alpha contamination | Internal exposure risk from alpha-emitting media | PAPR with P100 cartridges; fume hood; contamination monitoring |
| Safety — Photon emissions | Low-energy photon detectability constrains handling | Shielded storage; minimal unshielded handling time |
| Logistics — Procurement availability | Limited supply of ≥200 suitable detectors | Diversified sourcing; extended procurement window |
| Logistics — Batch inconsistency | Variable media activity / geometry across units | Pre-screening; batch characterization |
| Administrative — Bulk procurement thresholds | Orders >100 units/quarter may attract scrutiny | Phased procurement; documentation of research purpose |
| Administrative — Licensing delays | Delays in securing approvals may delay project | Early engagement with RSO; parallel planning activities |
| Perception — Anomalous procurement patterns | Unusual orders of moderating materials + shielding | Thorough documentation; academic research justification |
| Safety — Mechanical hazards | Press operations, chemical solvents | Machine guarding; chemical hygiene plans |
| Safety — Lead exposure | Pb–Bi alloy handling | Lead safety protocols; exposure monitoring |

